{% from "_chart.svg.j2" import chart %}
{#
  The canonical bilingual report page. Rendered once per governed language, so
  the root element states one `lang` and one `dir` and the stylesheet lays both
  directions out with logical properties.

  No expression here is exempted from autoescaping. Labels, caveats and
  commentary are customer- or provider-derived text and reach the reader
  escaped. The stylesheet is included as template source rather than passed in
  as a variable, which is what removes every reason to exempt anything: a page
  with one exemption in it has an escaping convention, not a guarantee.
#}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ language }}" dir="{{ direction }}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ chrome.title }}</title>
<style>
{% include stylesheet %}
</style>
{# Extension points for the surfaces that layer on this template. Empty here:
   this slice bundles no print stylesheet and no font binaries. #}
{% block embedded_fonts %}{% endblock %}
{% block print_stylesheet %}{% endblock %}
</head>
<body>
<a class="skip" href="#report">{{ chrome.skip }}</a>
<header>
<h1>{{ chrome.title }}</h1>
{#
  The disclosure, and not the narrative state beside it.
  `presentation-visibility-matrix.md` §A.4 classifies `narrative_state` as tier I,
  Internal: it says whether a provider produced prose, which means something only
  to someone *operating* Khepri. RRA-009 renders an Internal field on no customer
  surface, including the audit region, so it is not relocated -- it is simply not
  rendered. It remains on the bundle, and `_provenance` carries it into the audit
  region's provenance table where an auditor can read it as a governed field
  rather than as a hidden attribute on customer prose.
#}
<p class="disclosure" role="note">{{ disclosure }}</p>
</header>
<nav aria-label="{{ chrome.navigation }}">
<h2>{{ chrome.navigation }}</h2>
<ul>
{% for section in sections %}
<li><a href="#{{ section.section_id }}">{{ chrome.sections[section.section_id] }}</a></li>
{% endfor %}
<li><a href="#caveats">{{ chrome.caveats }}</a></li>
{% if passages %}
<li><a href="#commentary">{{ chrome.commentary }}</a></li>
{% endif %}
{#
  No citations or provenance entries: both sections moved to the audit region, and
  a navigation link to an anchor this document no longer contains is a dead link.
  The colophon is where a reader learns the evidence exists.
#}
</ul>
</nav>
<main id="report">
{% for section in sections %}
<section id="{{ section.section_id }}" aria-labelledby="{{ section.section_id }}-heading">
<h2 id="{{ section.section_id }}-heading">{{ chrome.sections[section.section_id] }}</h2>
{% if section.state == refused_state %}
<p class="refused">{{ chrome.refusal_prose[section.section_id][section.reason] }}</p>
{% else %}
{% if section.chart %}{{ chart(section.chart, section.section_id, chrome) }}{% endif %}
<div class="scroller" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="{{ chrome.business_caption }}">
<table class="figures">
<caption>{{ chrome.business_caption }}</caption>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">{{ chrome.business_figure }}</th>
<th scope="col">{{ chrome.value }}</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
{#
  Two columns, and no `data-figure-id`. The identifier columns -- metric, kind,
  unit, citation -- and the figure id itself all moved to the audit region, which
  `_evidence.html.j2` renders from the same bundle. Nothing is dropped: reconcile
  compares the surface *claim* and never this document, so relocation is
  claim-neutral while deletion would not be.

  **A row shows both its business name and its label when it has both**, and this
  is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. A bucket figure carries the customer's own
  product or branch in `label` while its metric supplies the name: `Attach rate`
  appears once per product, so showing the name alone renders several identical
  rows carrying different numbers -- unreadable, and a reader would be right to
  call it a misstatement. `Revenue change` needs `Against the previous period` for
  the same reason.

  Neither field alone is sufficient either. Five governed metrics carry no label at
  all and are named from the derived table, while a series row carries a period or
  product and no governed name. So: name, then label, then whichever exists.
#}
{% for cell in section.cells %}
<tr>
<th scope="row">
{%- if cell.metric_name and cell.label %}{{ cell.metric_name }} — {{ cell.label }}
{%- elif cell.metric_name %}{{ cell.metric_name }}
{%- else %}{{ cell.label }}{% endif -%}
</th>
<td class="figure">{{ cell.text }}</td>
</tr>
{% endfor %}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
{#
  A repeated series as a table rather than as rows. One row per label, one column
  per metric: a five-period series carrying revenue and units was ten rows before
  this, each repeating a metric name and stating one number, with the two halves
  of the comparison a reader wanted in different parts of the table.

  `_series_tables` decides the grouping and the column order, not this template --
  a template relating two metrics the bundle never related would be this surface
  inventing a series. An empty `<td>` is a metric carrying no figure at that
  label; it is deliberately not a zero, which would be a number nobody computed.
#}
{% for table in section.series %}
<div class="scroller" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="{{ chrome.series_caption }}">
<table class="figures figures--series">
<caption>{{ chrome.series_caption }}</caption>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">{{ chrome.series_label }}</th>
{% for heading in table.headings %}<th scope="col">{{ heading }}</th>
{% endfor %}
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
{% for row in table.rows %}
<tr>
<th scope="row">{{ row.label }}</th>
{% for text in row.texts %}<td class="figure">{{ text if text is not none else "" }}</td>
{% endfor %}
</tr>
{% endfor %}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% if section.caveats %}
<ul class="caveats caveats--section">
{% for code in section.caveats %}
<li>{{ caveat_prose[code] }}</li>
{% endfor %}
</ul>
{% endif %}
</section>
{% endfor %}
<section id="caveats" aria-labelledby="caveats-heading">
<h2 id="caveats-heading">{{ chrome.caveats }}</h2>
{% if caveats %}
<ul class="caveats">
{% for caveat in caveats %}
<li>{{ caveat_prose[caveat.code] }}</li>
{% endfor %}
</ul>
{% else %}
<p>{{ chrome.none }}</p>
{% endif %}
</section>
{% if passages %}
<section id="commentary" aria-labelledby="commentary-heading">
<h2 id="commentary-heading">{{ chrome.commentary }}</h2>
{#
  The prose, and not the fact identifiers it cites. `presentation-visibility-matrix.md`
  §A.4 classifies `passage.cited_fact_ids` as Audit: the passage text is already
  customer prose and is the one part of the original report that was, while the
  identifiers beside it mean something only to someone auditing Khepri. The
  passage-to-fact table is generated for every report in the audit region, so the
  commentary remains cited -- the citation is one region over, not absent.
#}
{% for passage in passages %}
<article id="passage-{{ passage.section_id }}">
<p>{{ passage.text }}</p>
</article>
{% endfor %}
</section>
{% endif %}
{#
  The citations list and the provenance table are gone from here, not gone from the
  report: `_evidence.html.j2` renders both, on the Technical Evidence page and in
  the printed appendix. Every citation and every identity field survives, one
  region over.
#}
<footer class="colophon">
<p>{{ chrome.colophon_reference }} {{ report_reference }}</p>
<p>{{ chrome.colophon_evidence }}</p>
</footer>
{#
  Filled by the print surface only. The screen surface publishes the audit region
  as a separate document, so a web reader who was not given the evidence page does
  not find it appended to the report they were given.
#}
{% block appendix %}{% endblock %}
</main>
</body>
</html>
